
  I recommend multi-user, server-backed, and only because of what you asked for. The notifications feature you described
  doesn't have a reason to exist in a single-user app. If the real situation is "solo app, I want reminders," say so —
  that's a much smaller system and I'd design it completely differently.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Who can change a task — how many users share the data? → Multi-user, server-backed (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Multi-user it is. Next question is about context rather than technology, because the stack answer falls out of it
  almost automatically.

  - [1mLearning/demo project[0m — the point is the code, not the users. I'd pick the smallest thing that's honestly
    multi-user: Node + SQLite, server-rendered HTML, session cookies, polling for notifications. No framework, no build
    step, deployable as one process. Everything stays readable.
  - [1mInternal tool, small team[0m — real people depend on it but you control the deployment. Same core, plus real auth,
    migrations, and a test suite that runs in CI. Notifications likely want email as well as in-app.
  - [1mProduct aimed at outside users[0m — changes the answer on auth, multi-tenancy, email deliverability, rate limiting, and
    notification preferences. Substantially more system, and several decisions become expensive to reverse.

  I recommend telling me it's the first unless it isn't. This started as an 11-line HTML file, and the cheapest good
  outcome is a small coherent system you can extend — over-building auth and email infrastructure for a demo is the most
  common way this kind of project stalls before notifications ever ship.
